cA-cU  (N)                    an empty rice-head; a panicle without a                                    kernel     cA-cS  (N)                    riceplant     cA=cY-nZ\  (N)                a variety of rice [PL]     cA-chC\  (N)                  hill-rice; strains of rice that grow in                                    mountain fields; non-glutinous rice          /SYN. cA-PC\ (N)/     cA jG\ ve  (OV)               thresh paddy          /SYN. cA dB\ ve (OV); see PLATES 26 and 27/          cA_jG\=qU-lu  (N)        flail for threshing paddy          cA-jG\=kH  (Ndeclaus)    threshing floor     cA=jN\-ma  (N)                a variety of rice [PL]     cA tZ\ ve  (OV)               carry threshed paddy back to the village                                    (on horseback)          /cf. cA pM ve (OV)/     cA ti ve  (OV)                plant paddy (one seed at a time); plant                                    one's crops          /SYN. cA qha ve (OV) [q.v.]; contrast cA P3 ve (OV)/          cA ti pF S te lZ qo dA\ jZ q>\-ma lC /  If they had only done it for                                    us right after the rice was planted it                                    would've been fine, wouldn't it!          cA ti pF ve tC PT qhe  (LRC + Nrh + Next)   around the morning after                                                       the rice-planting is                                                       finished          cA-ti=qa-e  (N)          dance performed at rice-planting time                                    [JHT:AKS]               /cf. cA-dB\=qa-e (N), cA-b@=qa-e (N)/     cA t3 ve  (OV)                pound paddy to remove husks          cA-t3=kH  (Ndeclaus)     rice-pounder; place where one husks the                                    paddy               /SYN. cA-t3=yS (N)/          cA-t3=cA\  (N)           rice-milling machine               /cf. ch@-k$ t3 ve (Nspec + V), ch@-k$ t3 tJ (Ndeclaus); SYN.                 hO-PI (N)/     cA_t9-lA\=qU  (N)             a variety of rice ("winged rice")                                                           ;     cA=tS\-nW  (N)                a variety of rice          /cf. tS\-nW (Mpfx) 'short and stubby'/     cA dB\ ve  (OV)               thresh paddy          /SYN. cA jG\ ve (OV)/          cA-dB\=qa-e  (N)         dance performed at rice-threshing time                                    [JHT:AKS]     cA-nu  (N)                    rice-panicle     cA-nB  (N)                    glutinous rice; sticky rice          /eaten occasionally by the Lahu in Thailand (esp. at New Year's),            though felt to be a Thai food; cf. Si. khZaw-ni0aw; particular            strains of glutinous rice include cA-nB=kh1w-hT (last element            < Thai "fragrant rice"), cA-nB=cQ-W or cA-nB=nZ\ [cf. nZ\ (Bn)            'black'], cA-nB=hT-W [cf. hT (Vadj) 'fragrant'], cA-nB=Pi [cf. Pi            (Bn) 'yellow'], cA-nB=lO(n) [cf. lO(n) (Bn) 'big']/          cA-nB=5  (N)             cooked sticky rice          cA-nB=qha  (N)           uncooked sticky rice     cA=pa-PQ  (N)                 a variety of rice [PL]     cA pM ve  (OV)                carry threshed paddy back to the village                                    (on people's backs)          /cf. cA tZ\ ve (OV)/     cA-p7  (N)                    rice-stoop; rice-cock; mound of paddy left                                    to dry in the fields for a couple of weeks                                    between harvesting and threshing          /SYN. cA-ph>\ (N); see PLATE 28/          cA tC p7  (NPq)          one mound of paddy          cA-p7 tC p7  (NPq)       one paddy-mound          cA-p7 ph>\ ve            pile up rice into stoops     cA=p>\-l>\  (N)               bamboo clangers several cords long [cf.                                   G-