es)          cA-PY=5 cZ ve  (OV)      celebrate the New Rice Feast          cA-PY-}H-PY  (Elabn)     "new rice and new water": the food from the                                    new harvest               /SYN. 5-PY-}H-PY (Elabn)/     cA-yD  (N)                    rice seedling          /SYN. hT-yD (N)/          cA-yD P3 kH  (Ndeclaus)  rice seedling nursery (where the seeds have                                    been sown broadcast)     cA yD\ ve  (OV)               thresh paddy with the feet     cA-yX(=W)  (N)                kind of small-grained rice          /SYN. cA-W (N)/     cA-yB  (N)                    rice seed          cA-yB n< p<\  (NPq)      two baskets of paddy seeds          cA-yB qha ve  (OV)       plant rice by hand          cA-yB k$ ve  (OV)        sow paddy (operation performed by women,                                    after the men have made the seedholes with                                    their dibbling-sticks)          cA-yB P3 ve  (OV)        sow rice seeds broadcast in a nursery     cA vB ve  (OV)                sift partially pounded rice          /SYN. cA }B ve (OV) [q.v.]/     cA-lG  (N)                    an enclosed area set off by bamboos or wood                                    inside a cA=c<-yS [q.v.]; the protected                                    part of a rice-storehouse          /cf. }Z\-lG (N) 'chicken-coop'/          cA-lG ti ve  (OV)        [RL] "plant a granary": to observe the                                    custom of leaving the last patch of cleared                                    field unplanted; the open space symbolizes                                    a granary to hold the hoped-for abundant                                    yield               /contrast cA lG ti ve (O + vV + Vh) 'beg smn to plant rice'                 [cf. lG (vV) 'beg']/          cA-lG te ve  (OV)        id. ("make a granary")     cA-lG  ~  cA-lwS  ~  cA-lw2  (N)  fast-maturing rice (strains of paddy                                        having a rapid growth, e.g. planted in                                        May and harvested in September)          /ANT. cA=kN-ma (N); cf. Si. khZaw-baw; particular strains of early-           maturing rice include cA-lG=qw3 (N), cA-lG=bJ\-S (N) [cf. bJ\-S            (Mpfx) 'sthg mottled'] and cA-lG=Pi (N) [cf. Pi (Bn) 'sthg            yellow']/                   STAGES IN THE MATURATION OF RICE-PLANTS     1. cA-yB P3 ve / cA-yD P3 ve / hT-yD P3 ve  (OV's)                                   sow the rice-seeds broadcast in nursery               2 months pass     2. hT-yD }G l@ ti-mi qh% qhAy ti ve  (Clnf + Clf)                                    pull up the rice-seedlings and transplant                                    them into the paddy-field               1 month passes     3. hT-dB phM\ ve  (SV)        "the riceplants turn": become viable green                                    plants about a cubit long               2 months pass     4. cA-9 9 la ve  (SV)         panicle-bearing shoots bud forth               2 weeks pass     5. G-nu qG\ pD\ tB\ la ve  (SV)  the panicles pop forth from the heads of                                       the shoots               and     6. ph^ qh% phS\ ve  (SV)      "[plants] come into the gray [phase]": the                                    flowers (G-vC\) and kernels (G-P8) appear,                                    and the whole field looks gray               a week or two pass     7. G-nu % la ve  (SV)         the panicles bend [under their own weight,                                    as the rice grains (cA-qha) grow big]               two or three weeks pass     8. cA m@ ve  (SV)             the paddy ripens (turning yellow)cA  (Vadj)                         be clever, skillful; be strong, active,                                  