hat he will be                                    stronger than they were          /cf. yZ ye ve (SV) 'child survives'; this is similar to the former            Jewish custom of naming a sickly-looking baby Alter (m.)/Alte (f.)            'old one', in hopes that it would live to grow old; see JAM 1979c/     cA-yG  (N)                    [cf. yG (N) 'sheep']     cA-fZ\  (N)                   [cf. fZ\ (N) 'rodent']          /name of an important Chinese-Lahu businessman in Farng/     cA-vA\  (N)                   [cf. vA\ (N) 'pig']          cA=vA\-W  (N)            [cf. vA\-W (N) 'piglet']     cA-lZ  (N)                    [cf. lZ (N) 'tiger']     cA-l<  (N)                    [cf. l< (N; Mpfx; Clf) 'custom, proper                                    behavior']     cA-l@  (N)                    name for last-born son [cf. l@ (V) 'be the                                    last']     cA-lB  (N)                    [cf. lB (Bn) 'dragon (in weekly animal                                    cycle)']          /name of chief Chiang Mai informant of 1965-6 fieldtrip, "Paul            Tcalo"; see Frontispiece photos/                                   2. prefix to names of male animals     /the following names are also usable for people, esp. in RL/     cA-nZ\  (N)                   Blackie [cf. nZ\ (N; Mpfx; Bn) 'black']          /name of one of author's cats, 1970-83/     cA-ni  (N)                    Rufus [cf. ni (Mpfx; Bn) 'red']     cA-phu  (N)                   Whitey [cf. phu (Mpfx; Bn) 'white']     cA-Pi  (N)                    Goldie; Brownie [cf. Pi (N; Bn) 'yellow;                                    brown']     /Note: the initial syllables in the following five entries, where cA       appears in names for animal species, are undoubtedly related to the cA       prefix used in male human and animal proper names; cf. Si. \Zj ~ \Zaj,       and Eng. expressions l